#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198021 :question/id #uuid "2a32501f-d0f2-5e59-b7cc-a6b459269f6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12357" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x, y, z) = \\left\\langle -y, \\: x, \\: z+2 \\right\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf F(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,x,\\,z+2⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2x)-(x)\\cdot (2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2y]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{7}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{7x}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198031 :question/id #uuid "c69a0f41-5b8f-5fcd-b058-94df2d7984bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12383" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle z+2y, \\: 3y+z, \\: x -z\\right\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-3x+2y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{0}^{1} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A}}} \\: \\text{d}v \\: \\text{d}u.\n$$\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: -3u+2v \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=-3u+2v} \\\\  & =⟨(-3u+2v)+2v,\\,3v+(-3u+2v),\\,u-(-3u+2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-3u+4v,\\,-3u+5v,\\,4u-2v⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-3u+4v)\\cdot (3)+(-3u+5v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-9u+12v+6u-10v+4u-2v \\\\  & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+2y,\\,3y+z,\\,x-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}u\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue}u}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198034 :question/id #uuid "b81eb0cb-a740-5e86-921c-4baa8c179e55" :question/math-academy-id "e-12387" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \n\\Bigg( \n\\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} \n+ Q \n- \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z}\n\\Bigg) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $\\mathbf j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x = - \\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z =   \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cea6d00-8d2b-5733-8616-9267687fd149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423087} {:answer/id #uuid "63293960-e3b1-529e-96e9-642aedf596e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423095} {:answer/id #uuid "84b21f44-ef97-5299-8654-bb5534b15960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423098} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8fdbef-d72d-513a-8e44-4a96d82e4382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}v-uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423108} {:answer/id #uuid "e83e93b0-2e8f-5324-b1ed-23d683ac8523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63293960-e3b1-529e-96e9-642aedf596e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423095} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab05865-9935-59ef-90a4-5cba4fa4f3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "412e405b-c9a9-5a1c-8e85-45f26189dce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-160887" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $-2\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨u+v-v,\\,2v-(u+v),\\,uv⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =u\\cdot (-1)+(v-u)\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-u-v+u+uv \\\\  & =uv-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(uv-v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}uv-v\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e4ed7d-32e1-561d-a22f-6872ac0c11d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276093} {:answer/id #uuid "79d3f9e1-b09d-5da0-9956-625389bb5691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276584} {:answer/id #uuid "7fce5fdb-d2d4-56fd-b48c-ac35d9caf62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276636} {:answer/id #uuid "dba9155e-47c3-5323-95b0-44b98e26ea32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277400} {:answer/id #uuid "eed29fd5-8470-5433-bc50-626d03bc72f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e4ed7d-32e1-561d-a22f-6872ac0c11d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276093} :answer-field/id #uuid "826fd7b3-ffdf-520e-95fa-e603158a44c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd29749c-bfb5-5726-8ec7-04f9f714fe37" :question/math-academy-id "q-160888" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\,2y+(x-y),\\,xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{xy}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae2d92e-ed1f-5f54-9f3e-4ce38b8da70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂P,\\,$ $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275570} {:answer/id #uuid "2786ad1c-2c77-5044-8bba-022ff896dea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275839} {:answer/id #uuid "44ac2a5e-f444-5ab8-8d2f-b1befe6ae68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276081} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5bb47b-fd98-5623-8225-b6a60c57026d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276170} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ef7c7-aca0-5aa4-b9ce-95d7314ff4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ef7c7-aca0-5aa4-b9ce-95d7314ff4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276743} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e5c477-cfc8-5f60-94c2-948eec13a409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9923f940-6e67-51d8-8336-d46f1ce02fd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161166" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05adf01e-c7b1-5e97-b5eb-3f2025764ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275527} {:answer/id #uuid "54638f1d-1303-5a95-ba48-da06bfbcee4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276226} {:answer/id #uuid "57c6c235-25f5-5f19-a313-895f424e6c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $P$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276263} {:answer/id #uuid "74d9839f-a860-523e-923f-a6741e1f1d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276535} {:answer/id #uuid "f604d50d-71c4-5c4d-a5df-37fe3a24fd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f604d50d-71c4-5c4d-a5df-37fe3a24fd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277629} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de77bc5-7a01-5bd2-8ed1-6d5e7458e367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8aa780b-8e8f-52b7-b96a-66254bcb10c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161189" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f754a120-8886-56d3-b535-398f13b91c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398529} {:answer/id #uuid "d28df103-ae23-5647-a506-1bc60c332868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+uv+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398530} {:answer/id #uuid "e85142cb-e059-512c-8b7b-18eee7a8c253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398531} {:answer/id #uuid "e433762d-59a0-5efb-bef2-7687d8583f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398532} {:answer/id #uuid "3a53a3c7-e2f5-50e9-afd4-d3b986017cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(u+v)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e85142cb-e059-512c-8b7b-18eee7a8c253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398531} :answer-field/id #uuid "185487e5-090f-5815-93b2-dfc0c983e691" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3bcb9d3-050c-5ab9-bd2a-436465223147" :question/math-academy-id "q-161360" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,{z}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}+2uv+{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198615 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06423401-c4cb-59a0-9bd6-7e1a82b5b0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275534} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce5551e-7454-5d22-ae6a-ac13e583b0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275587} {:answer/id #uuid "1481a58c-6133-58f7-a21d-cabc8d7625ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275668} {:answer/id #uuid "7671f819-6d07-506b-b49f-13d0ef633588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276546} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab9fbd0-cdfd-51f8-ab82-86979d2a493d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1481a58c-6133-58f7-a21d-cabc8d7625ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275668} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed331e7d-c8f9-522c-8244-24e655efebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56a2b4c8-31ce-51dd-93f5-6f1083f199f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161553" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $F$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩{|}_{z=-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{e}^{x}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{{e}^{y}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{1-x-y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({e}^{x})\\cdot (-1)-({e}^{y})\\cdot (-1)+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{x}y+{e}^{y}+y-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+e+1-x-\\frac{1}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-x-\\frac{1}{2}+e)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+ex]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+e-1 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} {:answer/id #uuid "62de7813-7886-5bed-86cd-1ca23e600b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21{x}^{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513799} {:answer/id #uuid "65428549-ccd7-5201-8026-265c549f0352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513804} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af8b6-1444-5218-bbb1-bf6a887d157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{19}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513863} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf69104-089b-5eec-8f81-3bf53d5632ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{x}^{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} :answer-field/id #uuid "6982cbcb-0bd9-554d-8104-e02dd398a383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e6c66f6-6133-59f6-a79d-0cbf1615accf" :question/math-academy-id "q-113665" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} \\\\ &= {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233175 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7949e7bf-09d1-5ce9-ac3c-0d7f26b2e6c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-113670" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{17}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=17$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{17}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{17+1}}{17+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{18})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 18\\,{x}^{17} \\\\ &= {x}^{17}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233176 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353f4779-4251-5a93-b99f-1eda6757b24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113674" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233177 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1eab1e18-d0df-58e6-917b-50a1d74ed3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-113680" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5}}{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}{x}^{8/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233178 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63703f37-ab84-53aa-922b-b2e419a9f1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113695" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{2/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/5+1}}{(\\frac{2}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{2/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233179 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25edeff2-faa4-50c4-bbfd-3291d7ac7694" :question/math-academy-id "q-113705" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}={x}^{-4/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{1/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233325 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "023f3e70-1c18-56f9-a0dc-bbd6d23f44df" :question/math-academy-id "q-123754" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t-4,\\,\\,\\,y=1-2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 4,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $b$ | $c$ | $-31$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=t-4.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=0-4=-4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=1-2{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=1-2(2)^{2}=-7.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=1-2{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=1-2(3)^{2}=-17\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+(-7)+(-17)=-28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233364 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a558acd-0b05-5aa8-b518-45323798923a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125086" :question/problem "Find $a+3b$ if $a=8i-5j$ and $b=-3i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a+3b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3b & =(8i-5j)+3(-3i+2j) \\\\  & =8i-5j-9i+6j \\\\  & =(8i-9i)+(-5j+6j) \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233365 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d15383e5-4c36-54f6-9a69-8b61467023e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125088" :question/problem "Find $3a-2b$ if $a=-2i-2j$ and $b=4i-3j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $3a-2b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-2b & =3(-2i-2j)-2(4i-3j) \\\\  & =-6i-6j-8i+6j \\\\  & =(-6i-8i)+(-6j+6j) \\\\  & =-14i+0j \\\\  & =-14i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d273204-9c71-5dd0-b69b-1a90276ae151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d273204-9c71-5dd0-b69b-1a90276ae151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716952} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37edfe3-8d04-5659-9550-117d4c4acd83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed3ae951-db6d-557c-8a34-9c013751cf9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127614" :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233382 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4885183-19e5-5c7a-bc09-9d2bdd330428" :question/math-academy-id "q-1300" :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨-3,1⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨4,6⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =⟨-3,1⟩+⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3+4,1+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233383 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab7db4c4-9361-5a49-9950-d14e7b984f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-1301" :question/problem "What is the sum of $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨10,5⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{B}=⟨8,-2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨10,5⟩+⟨8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233395 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c85fb6aa-50dc-5e02-b59e-421805ee7c0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-1324" :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{U}=⟨1,3⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{V}=⟨6,3⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V} & =⟨1,3⟩+⟨6,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+6,3+3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233442 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "875b5e0a-1731-539f-8cf3-8903a8a62d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-1389" :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$, then $-4\\overset{\\to }{A}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $-4\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-4\\cdot ⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-4)\\cdot 4,(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-16,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "716b9760-7403-5664-a541-6f49fb28dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716b9760-7403-5664-a541-6f49fb28dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e017c6d-4e9c-5cb6-a364-f311476d6242" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a718be7-eb73-54f8-925e-728120665d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-143041" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26795cb5-46f0-593d-bbbf-be3f06b14875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -4,\\,y\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275826} {:answer/id #uuid "324e6b9f-c15b-5458-9243-fbb2407d28b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,y\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275921} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fae690-6db1-517e-a307-69dfa66a5f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -\\frac{1}{2},\\,y\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276927} {:answer/id #uuid "b1310550-084d-5243-93a9-5a121ec3fd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277063} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cbc30c-7bf6-5ea9-9861-98cf564bf084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1310550-084d-5243-93a9-5a121ec3fd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277063} :answer-field/id #uuid "adfdb777-9023-51f2-b4b7-4925f3b6bdcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c5d5a76-6d19-5d2e-8ac8-15cac8c3ca23" :question/math-academy-id "q-16149" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-2,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}+t \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0.$ Therefore, we can conclude that $y\\ge -2.$\n\nSo, the ranges are $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}$ and $y\\ge -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca60fdcc-dda9-5409-bb81-344b09986ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca60fdcc-dda9-5409-bb81-344b09986ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232410} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca56b8e5-8405-5ba4-ada6-394dce99dea5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232409} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eb58f9-5c4d-5704-b1f3-24df3d108cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03eb58f9-5c4d-5704-b1f3-24df3d108cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232412} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d59c0f-f0c5-5b07-bebb-1320bc54f3be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a7e0156-a9f9-52f5-af28-277e2c6df336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7e0156-a9f9-52f5-af28-277e2c6df336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232414} :answer-field/id #uuid "225a0b88-6817-55d6-a3db-6d411d484997" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957fdecf-4769-50cf-b898-f2272fca2edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "957fdecf-4769-50cf-b898-f2272fca2edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232416} :answer-field/id #uuid "61313d60-2a62-55d9-b1c5-2d9707ea6eb2" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2ef4612-07ce-57c4-8427-f0e2c2f142da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ef4612-07ce-57c4-8427-f0e2c2f142da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232418} :answer-field/id #uuid "4531d739-aa50-5c78-a314-c0d516f7b7b2" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232417} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc6ca0f1-8049-59b6-9424-d6d0d4f4577d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc6ca0f1-8049-59b6-9424-d6d0d4f4577d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232420} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c71493-7df0-5a0d-b2fb-741a03aba773" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbb8764c-a924-5241-b809-c578415b87f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-276258" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2\\le y\\le 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233751 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a192223-274d-5d58-847a-c92d2faa98df" :question/math-academy-id "q-29633" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233752 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dcd24a2-ce5f-5ab6-8a92-814e8f1355a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-29662" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}={x}^{-5/2}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} & =\\int {x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233753 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07729051-76ae-508a-bad7-395a080dc6f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-29745" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(-10)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2-50 \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233754 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70c1a973-bd6b-5c29-b4c9-891bb77cdee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-29750" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2+1}}{-\\frac{1}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2{x}^{1/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}-1) \\\\  & =2(2-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
